---
layout: null
sitemap:
  exclude: true
---
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom">
  <channel>
    <title>{{ site.title | xml_escape }}</title>
    <description>{{ site.description | xml_escape }}</description>
    <link>{{ '/' | absolute_url }}</link>
    <language>en</language>
    <atom:link href="{{ '/feed.xml' | absolute_url }}" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml" />
    <pubDate>{{ site.time | date_to_rfc822 }}</pubDate>
    <lastBuildDate>{{ site.time | date_to_rfc822 }}</lastBuildDate>
    <generator>Jekyll v{{ jekyll.version }}</generator>
    {% comment %}
      The search overlay consumes this same feed. Keeping only twenty items made
      every older article silently disappear from on-site search once the family
      archive grew past that threshold. Descriptions keep the feed compact, so the
      complete post list is both a valid RSS archive and a complete search index.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% for post in site.posts %}
      <item>
        <title>{{ post.title | xml_escape }}</title>
        {% comment %}
          `<description>` previously carried `post.content` — the entire rendered article,
          markup and all, escaped into one attribute-sized blob. That made the feed
          enormous and, more concretely, broke the search overlay: super-search.js
          matches against title and description, so with the full body in there every
          query matched every post. A real summary goes in description; the full body
          goes in content:encoded, where readers expect it.
        {% endcomment %}
        <description>{{ post.description | default: post.subtitle | default: post.excerpt | strip_html | normalize_whitespace | truncate: 300 | xml_escape }}</description>
        <pubDate>{{ post.date | date_to_rfc822 }}</pubDate>
        <link>{{ post.url | absolute_url }}</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">{{ post.url | absolute_url }}</guid>
        {% for tag in post.tags %}
        <category>{{ tag | xml_escape }}</category>
        {% endfor %}
        {% for cat in post.categories %}
        <category>{{ cat | xml_escape }}</category>
        {% endfor %}
      </item>
    {% endfor %}
  </channel>
</rss>
